s.\" —Michael Kazin, New York Times Book Review The transcontinental railroads were the first corporate behemoths. Their attempts to generate profits from proliferating debt sparked devastating economic panics. Their dependence on public largesse drew them into the corridors of power, initiating new forms of corruption. Their operations rearranged space and time, remade the landscape of the West, and opened new ways of life and work. Their discriminatory rates sparked a new antimonopoly politics. The transcontinentals were pivotal actors in the making of modern America, but the triumphal myths of the golden spike, Robber Barons larger than life, and an innovative capitalism all die here. Instead we have a new vision of the Gilded Age, often darkly funny, that shows history to be rooted in failure as well as success.","subjectCategories":[{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","label":"History","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-railroaded-the-transcontinentals-and-the-making-of-modern-america-richard-white"}],"displaySummary":"The transcontinentals were pivotal actors in the making of modern America, but the triumphal myths of the golden spike, Robber Barons larger than life, and an innovative capitalism all die here. Instead we have a new vision of the Gilded Age, often darkly funny, that shows history to be rooted in failure as well as success.","primaryTopic":"Africa & the African Diaspora","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"American History","confidence":"medium","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"American History","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"American History","mappedSubject":"American History","score":8,"confidence":"high","note":"The book primarily examines the impact of transcontinental railroads on American society and politics during the Gilded Age, making American History the dominant subject, while also touching on governance and economic aspects.","id":"subjec